#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172953} :activity/id #uuid "62e770c1-74a2-50e2-bdb5-2e349068a391" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049412 :topic/id #uuid "f25812af-5efe-5f1b-8645-0d3d44e562fd" :topic/math-academy-id 3292} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172953 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173183 :tutorial/content "We can use the following property to simplify expressions involving expected values:\n\n> For any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b$, we have\n> $\\textrm E[aX+bY] = a \\textrm E[X] + b \\textrm E[Y]$.\n\nIn other words, the expected value of a sum of scaled random variables equals the sum of the scaled expected values.\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[2X+5Y] = 2 \\textrm E[X] + 5 \\textrm E[Y]\n$$\n\nNote that the property also works with subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[2X-5Y] = 2 \\textrm E[X] - 5 \\textrm E[Y]\n$$\n\nWe can extend this idea to any number of random variables. For example, for any random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$, and real constants $a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[a_1X_1+a_2X_2+\\cdots+ a_nX_n]= a_1\\textrm E[X_1]+a_2\\textrm E[X_2]+\\cdots +a_n\\textrm{E}[X_n]\n$$\n\nor more concisely,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\left[\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i X_i\\right] =\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i \\textrm E[X_i]\n$$\n\nNote that the random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$, are *not* required to be independent." :tutorial/id #uuid "abd7e05d-2ae3-5150-bb4c-9c91ccac9917" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5438 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1f575493-d240-5973-bc11-3b5c2d96dfe2" :step/math-academy-id 33854 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172954}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172954 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172067 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171873 :question/id #uuid "24cbb085-f4b4-5bdb-add2-3ba78337f37f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9080"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "272baac4-5bd1-5539-9493-27e9728fa7eb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050775}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172240}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Expected Value of a Sum of Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "3d111675-db86-50cd-9a8d-bdac4857566b" :step/math-academy-id 33855 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172956}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172955 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172068 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171874 :question/id #uuid "f69db820-d94c-5c04-a3e2-34ad50091d67" :question/math-academy-id "e-9081"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f205e5f7-b2f0-5daa-9392-dc627a5cb277" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050835}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172248}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Expected Value of a Product of Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "bdc0cf78-f933-51de-b80d-dca746f566da" :step/math-academy-id 33862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172956 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172069 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171875 :question/id #uuid "1c91327d-01a2-5937-a26d-741205897a28" :question/math-academy-id "e-9085"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbf1aa8a-50e6-5fff-bcde-8d621ad8daad" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172241}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Expected Value of a Sum of Random Variables in Context"} :step/id #uuid "f70bac75-68c2-5cbe-9e3a-72c3e3ef1fef" :step/math-academy-id 33869 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172966}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172966 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173187 :tutorial/content "The expected value of the product of two *independent* random variables is equal to the product of the expected values. So, if the random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X\\cdot Y] = \\textrm E[X]\\cdot \\textrm E[Y]\n$$\n\nFor example, if we know that $X$ are $Y$ are independent, and that\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X]=2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[Y]=3\n$$\n\n, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X \\cdot Y] &= E[X] \\cdot E[Y] \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen working with the expected value of a product of random variables, it's important to check that the variables are independent. The formula above is *not* true if $X$ and $Y$ are not independent.\n\nMore generally, if the random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ are *mutually* independent, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X_1\\cdot X_2 \\cdots X_n]=\\textrm E[X_1]\\cdot \\textrm E[X_2]\\cdot\\ldots\\cdot \\textrm E[X_n]\n$$\n\nor more concisely,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\left[ \\prod_{i=1}^n X_i\\right] = \\prod_{i=1}^n \\textrm E[X_i]\n$$\n\nwhere $\\prod$ is the product operator." :tutorial/id #uuid "82a0ae48-7d46-5109-82c6-528ead8fa8d8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5511 :tutorial/title "The Expectation of a Product of Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "3717594a-2556-5b59-83db-8060ffe1072e" :step/math-academy-id 34335 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172955}}] :activity/title "Expected Values of Sums and Products of Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172143}]}
